<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Netherlands New Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1949 CE – 1962 CE · capital Hollandia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Last Dutch colony in Asia, the western half of New Guinea, whose transfer to Indonesia remains contested by Papuan nationalists.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> in 1949, the status of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> was left for later talks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> kept it as Netherlands New Guinea, arguing that its Papuan peoples were culturally distinct from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> claimed it as part of the former colony.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapura">Hollandia</a>, the colony saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> investment in schools, missions and administration aimed at preparing Papuans for self-rule. A partly elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> Council met in 1961, and on 1 December 1961 the Morning Star flag was raised alongside the Dutch flag. Most Papuans lived in villages, and Christian missions had wide influence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno's</a> military campaign and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Agreement in August 1962. The territory passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> administration and then to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in 1963, confirmed by the disputed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-free-choice">Act of Free Choice</a> in 1969.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/netherlands-new-guinea.jpg" alt="Netherlands New Guinea" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Netherlands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1949 CE – 1962 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hollandia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jan van Baal, Pieter Platteel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, Malay, Papuan languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dutch New Guinea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=netherlands-new-guinea" aria-label="Netherlands New Guinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8587 2272 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Netherlands New Guinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8907 2528L7959 2663M8907 2528L5110 626M8907 2528L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0M5110 626h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8907 2528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8907 2528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=netherlands-new-guinea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
